      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Marie Antoinette did not have a standardized personal appearance across all portraits, as her style evolved significantly from her domestic years in the court to her later, more austere period before the revolution. Art historians note that her most famous likeness, often associated with the \"Rouge Carmose\" or her 1778 portrait by Rosalba Carriera, features distinct makeup including extensive rosy lips painted with rouge and a constrained, high-necked crinoline silhouette rather than modern \"way of moving\" tells. While these specific fashion elements could easily create a lingering impression in a crowded downtown cafe, there is no historical evidence of her retaining specific physical traits beyond general royal aesthetics that would allow one to identify her truly without direct recognition by the people who knew her.",
